WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:07.700
So to jump right
into things, I'm

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.090
going to ask a few questions.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:12.960
And then if it applies to you,
we're going to clap twice.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.790
So clap twice if you've
ever written a Shiny app.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.090
Sweet, awesome.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:19.920
That's a lot of you.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.200
Good! All right.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.295
Clap twice if you remember
all of your app's features.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.810
(no, or very few, claps)
Okay... and then...

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:35.050
Clap twice if you would feel
comfortable having a coworker

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.290
rewrite part of your app.
(no, or very few, claps)

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.640
Ah, still not a lot of
you, but most of us

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.640
feel about our
apps, it's our precious,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.840
We don't want anyone
else to touch it.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.660
So this gives is like,
as Sydeaka was saying,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.290
with automated testing as well.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.160
And having other working
with team members,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.800
this brings a big
motivation to have something

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.100
with automated testing.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.050
So that we don't lose
our app features.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.230
Or we could have a coworker
at work on a new feature

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.010
and not have it destroy
your existing features.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.440
And so we'll get into
this throughout today.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:10.940
All right.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.773
So to make sure everyone's
on the same page,

00:01:12.773 --> 00:01:15.950
even though there's
lots of claps, what is Shiny.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.100
So Shiny allows you
to quickly build

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.330
full stack interactive
web applications.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.270
And it does this
in very few lines.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.360
I believe, for you to do
this in other languages,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.650
it would take a lot of work.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.010
And it does this by executing
reactive expressions

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.490
within the server and the server,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.130
when you change one
of these reactive values,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.310
all downstream reactions (are)
invalidated.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.000
And then they must
be recalculated.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.370
And this is how we can do
a lot of our reactivity

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.700
within Shiny.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.630
And as Sydeaka said, there's
this very familiar workflow

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.098
within Shiny.

00:01:56.098 --> 00:01:58.140
It's where we're going to
tweak some of the apps

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.890
we're going to add and adjust
some of that reactivity,

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.080
we'll then click
Run app and then -

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.610
this is why we love R -
that we manually look at it.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.670
We can manually
iterate on this and we

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.820
can do this every 30
seconds, 10 seconds

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.140
even, if you're really quick.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.520
And that's why we love
R. (With) JavaScript,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.690
it might be 10 hours if you want
to add a new feature. With R,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.550
if you know the commands it could
just be a few seconds.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.860
And that's great.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.010
But, what if you have 20 apps?

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.180
What if you have
many team members?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.680
What if you have things
where you can't quite keep it

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.300
all in your head at once?

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:37.160
And with manually experimenting,
things are lost in translation.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.070
Can you explain to your
coworker all of your features

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.080
and have them make sure they
manually test it properly?

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.420
Like, I don't believe
I could do that.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.670
And I'm very familiar
with the infrastructure.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.250
And so it kind of
crumbles once you

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:55.010
start doing more than
just toy applications.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.370
And Gimli says there's no 
chance of success.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.010
So this kind of opens the
door to a different package.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.710
And this is the
"test that" package.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:06.210
The "test that"

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.540
package is typically more
from the package development

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.320
world of and it performs
unit testing for R

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.140
And this is where it executes
formal unit test such as "expect

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.390
equal" or "expect greater than".

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.820
And I think this is great.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.480
You can build your
whole test suite

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.890
and then build out
your functionality

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.950
to try to pass that test suite.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.440
I remember having many
situations where we might

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.620
try to build out 100 tests.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.810
Then let's build those methods
to try to pass those tests.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.780
Test-driven development.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.750
And its goals are
four main goals.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.550
Number one, to have fewer bugs.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.170
If you're constantly
testing things,

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:48.020
then things get broken
less often because you're

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.390
very aware of what's going on.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:54.050
You can also have more robust
code because maybe you're

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.270
testing for chicken fingers.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.850
That's not something that would
automatically test by default

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.370
during my manual test.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.470
If you have better code
structure as you're

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.520
breaking your functions down.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.540
If you test each LEGO
piece of your code,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.600
then when you put all that
together, you can believe

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.390
that you have a strong
code structure at the end,

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.910
very robust because you
know, each component

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.310
is doing very well.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:22.160
And then finally you
have call to action.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.860
Right now, I am getting
very little sleep.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.080
I have a new baby boy.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.140
And it's wonderful having
call to action because -

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.160
Oh, Thank you -

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.185
It's like you can
set in a broken test

00:04:35.185 --> 00:04:36.810
and say, like, fix
this in the morning

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.390
and then you don't need that
45 minutes kind of warm up

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.000
for the day.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.610
You can kind of jump back in 
because you

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:47.180
had that test while you were
deep into your coding session.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.820
So let's take a look at "test that"
and kind of see what an example is.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.838
I talked about
those formal tests.

00:04:52.838 --> 00:04:54.380
This would be like
expect equal where

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.210
we can do our standard,
basic test, 2 times 2.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.290
Does it equal four?

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.290
Yes, great.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:01.485
Kind of a non-standard one.

00:05:01.485 --> 00:05:02.860
Make it a little
bit more robust.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.380
2 times NA that's an NA
integer, if you do NA regular,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.280
that's actually illogical
and it would fail

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.970
without the underscore integer.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:16.690
And then new to "test that"
edition 3 is snapshot testing.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:18.550
Snapshot testing
is where we don't

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.780
do an expect equal x to y.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.290
We say kind of keep
track of this file

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.410
and then just make
sure it doesn't change

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.860
because that file may
contain lots of content that

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.110
can't be put into code.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.420
So we can make an
example file and then

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.030
we can say expect
snapshot file from "test

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.700
that" and it will compare
to, say, did the file change

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.770
from each run, Yes or No.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:46.470
And if it didn't,
then the test passed.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.130
So if we run devtools
test within the IDE,

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.080
you can see that those three
tests pass very quickly.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.590
And you get a
success and "test that"

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.510
does a wonderful job
of encouragement.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:01.420
But that was a bad ass code.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.910
You test over and over again.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.220
Get a new random message every
(inaudible)

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.730
So I'd like to take
these two worlds

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.210
and combine them into one.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.340
And here's what
we'll get: shinytest2.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.270
The shinytest2 is unit
testing for Shiny applications.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.170
But I actually want to adjust
that a little bit, because it's

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:29.840
very difficult to write
all of the unit tests

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.820
and then fill in the logic.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:36.700
Instead, it's more regression
testing for Shiny applications.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.580
In that you have
existing app behavior

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:42.690
and you've seen this through
your own manual testing.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.050
And you don't want to lose this.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.607
Typically we do something
like three hours of work

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:49.190
and I want to make
sure that you don't

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:50.460
lose that three hours of work.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.970
So we're going to insert
a test and make sure

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:57.220
that behavior does not
regress, or does not go away.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.680
It executes within "test that",

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.640
so there's a framework
that we're all hopefully

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:01.820
very familiar with.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:05.660
And it should be very
natural as to what happens. 

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:09.230
In shinytest, the
extra helper methods

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:11.270
that exist within
there, they all

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:15.060
leverage to "test
that" snapshot file.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.980
So there's nothing special
that shinytest is doing.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.730
It's just kind of
orchestrating everything

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:23.310
into a nice place packed.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.530
And then, especially
important, it's

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.920
built on chromote, which uses
your local chromium based

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.810
browser, typically Chrome,
and allows you to execute

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.470
headless browser tests.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:36.970
So it doesn't need to open up.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:39.510
It can execute just
as GitHub action

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.693
or even hidden in your terminal.

00:07:42.693 --> 00:07:44.110
But one of the
beautiful things is

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.120
that you can view that
live test application.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.130
And I'll show a
demo of that later.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:51.260
And then also because
it's Chrome, you

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:54.050
can have your familiar
debugging tools

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:58.610
that Chrome provides and
those are very, very powerful.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.110
So elephant in the room.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.280
What about

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:02.980
Shinytest?

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.970
If you are familiar with it,
it's entering maintenance mode.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.480
The headless browser
has been set on end

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.200
of life for a few years now.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:12.810
PhantomJS

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.280
is not really usable.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.490
I actually would argue
it is worse than IE,

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.240
and IE has already
been killed by Microsoft.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.670
And also Shiny is
moving towards wanting

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:25.550
to have everyone use
Bootstrap 5 and it is not

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.170
Bootstrap 5 compatible.

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:31.300
So it is impossible
to do these tests.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.730
So shinytest2,

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:36.470
also, it has a different
headless browser.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:39.530
So all of your prior
screenshots, if you had them,

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.390
they would be a failure.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.680
And that's not something that
I want to introduce on everyone.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.260
Also because of "test that"
we have a different file

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.080
structure,

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.100
and since we had the
opportunity we also

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:55.870
streamlined the API to leverage
test that, as well as a different 

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:57.888
R API. Well that's why
there's two different.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.120
So before we get into
some deeper code,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:08.650
let's look at a very small
toy example, Shiny app.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:10.080
And what it's going to do, 

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.300
it's going to ask your name.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.880
We're then going
to greet that name

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.660
and then we should have
a response or a greeting.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.930
I'll insert my name
Barrett and click greet.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:22.950
It'll say "Hello, Barrett!"

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:23.730
That's it.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.150
Very, very complicated.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:26.010
I know.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.620
But it's nice because
we have a text input,

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.410
we have an action button
and we also have a text out.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.080
These are very common components
within your Shiny application,

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.580
and they may be located in
very different locations,

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.820
but these are a good
example of what's happening.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.270
If I was to set
that up as a test,

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:50.270
we would need to set up
our own "test that" code.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.570
So we would say "test that"
the app says "hello Barrett",

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:57.060
and this is in the file there.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.283
And if we were to insert
some shinytest2 to code,

00:09:59.283 --> 00:10:00.700
you don't need to
know it exactly.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.990
But we can kind of
walk through it.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.610
We're here inside the test, 

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:05.808
we will set up our AppDriver.

00:10:05.808 --> 00:10:07.600
This is the thing that
kind of orchestrates

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.580
everything - gets chromote going,
get your Shiny app going

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.250
and then it handles
all the interactions.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.650
And then we tell the
app, hey, set the input

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.810
and say name is Barrett.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:21.680
Then when that's all done,
click the greet button and then

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.440
when everything has
been done as well,

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.750
say expect values
because I want make

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:29.930
sure my input and output are 
consistent.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:31.400
And I think it's
just so beautiful

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.730
that in four lines of code, I
set up the app, set some input

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:36.170
and make expectations.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.960
And it's not a crazy thing.

00:10:40.705 --> 00:10:42.080
So let's run that
test because we

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:44.880
want to run these tests
over and over and rapidly,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.130
constantly. In real time.

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:49.340
Took a few seconds.

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.440
Not too bad.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.200
Now, your three hours of work
that you kind of worked on

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.250
is now captured in being able
to replay in a few seconds.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.210
Make sure that
work is never lost.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.350
"But writing tests is hard."

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:05.935
says everyone but Hadley.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:13.760
The good news is, is there's
a method called record test.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.680
And I think this is
great because you don't

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.160
need to write those tests.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.210
So we can capture all of
the Shiny interactions

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:21.820
and then record that as a "test

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.150
that" test.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.640
And I really want to stress this
because I love the McDonald's

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.670
quote of "if you have time to
clean, you have time to clean."

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:33.520
But in this case, "if
you have time to rest,

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.950
you have time to record a test."

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:40.760
And this is because
recording these tests

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.580
is not that long when you
do your manual testing.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.000
It takes just a few
seconds, 10 seconds.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.730
This whole gif here
is just 10 seconds.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:50.390
So it's a very
familiar greeting.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:51.900
We do the interactions.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.630
And then I click the
values and I save and exit.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:12:00.360
That's it. The 10 seconds
for me to go in,

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.240
do the manual
manipulations that I want,

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.270
I then save my Shiny values
or expect my Shiny values

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.710
and I save it.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.062
And I think it's just great.

00:12:09.062 --> 00:12:10.770
And all of your app
will live on the side

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:14.340
and you can see your code update
as to what being generated.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.280
And then when we're all done
after next, at least one

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.960
expectation made, then we save.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:25.480
Great that code
that was recorded,

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.760
this is actually what is stored.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.360
So I didn't doctor this one and
it is oddly similar to the one

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:32.570
that we looked at before.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:35.320
Just the only difference
is the test name

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:39.300
is just saying that
it's the recording.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.230
Let's look at that
testing we want.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:44.850
I want to know that always
run your DevTools run these

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:48.220
faster like constantly, because
they are fast and quick.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.270
Not too bad.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:51.460
And I want to make sure
that you don't lose any.

00:12:55.153 --> 00:12:56.570
But remember earlier
I was talking

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:59.350
about how we had chromote.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:01.820
And in chromote you can view your
headless browser.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.320
If you're familiar
with Shiny tests.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:04.630
It was a black box.

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:07.960
Could only poke it from
the outside and pray.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:09.958
There is not much you could do.

00:13:09.958 --> 00:13:12.250
With our apps, they are getting
more and more complicated,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:13.490
and that's awesome.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.690
It's wonderful to see.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.340
But if it's a
headless browser, it

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:20.730
means you can't see it like
our regular browsers today,

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.230
but chromote allows
you to view it.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:26.010
So there's method you
can run interactively.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.990
Called app$ 
or $view.

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:31.940
And this is what it will.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.880
So in this gif...I have
the test on the right

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.340
that I'm running
interactively in my console.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:40.100
And what we'll do is we
will library(shinytest2)

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:44.960
I will then run my AppDriver
working directory app;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:46.300
I'll app$view.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:50.020
And this will open up Chrome
browser on your screen.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:53.090
You can see the app there and
you can, on the right side,

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.880
you can also see all
of your familiar

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.390
Chrome debugging tools.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:57.060
Just great.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:01.240
And also see your console at the
bottom, shinytest will spit

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:02.380
out some information about

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:03.410
what's happening.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.860
&lt;inaudible&gt; if
I call set_input,

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:07.500
you can see the app update live.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:08.750
It didn't have to do anything,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.620
you didn't have to refresh.
And when we click greet

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:14.240
it actually updated
live you see the output

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.960
there. I think that is
just so powerful because you

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.310
can be like: "why is
this test not working?"

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:21.550
Run, run, run, run.

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:25.700
Insert an app$view in the console,
then be exactly where it's at

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:29.930
and you know your app and you'll be
able to discover what's wrong.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.440
Whereas shinytest2 you can
only tell you what's going on.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:39.340
So I have some suggestions
as you leave the Shiny Shire

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:43.220
and you are on your way to 
testing.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.790
#1 is to use
shiny::exportTestValues()

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:49.260
This is a method that I
was actually unaware of.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.900
I'm on the Shiny
team and I really

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:55.530
want to give it more limelight
because it is very powerful.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:58.090
We don't need to test
just inputs and outputs.

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:00.740
There's actually a third
group called Export

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.390
and you can export
react values.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.120
You can export your
reactive value,

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:07.760
and it doesn't need to be
an input or something that

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.530
has been rendered as an output.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.900
This is great
because testing mode

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:14.600
and will allow for
this to work and you

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.810
can expose more information
and more working logic that

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:22.190
doesn't need to be in your normal
app that you use day to day.

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:25.760
Also try to limit objects
under your own control.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.730
If you do something like a
data structure and then you

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:32.900
send it into DT and DT
updates because it needs to.

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:35.810
Nothing wrong with DT doing
that, but your output

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.300
might change, which would
produce false positives.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:41.810
So maybe just test your
data frame going in

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:47.680
and then just trust
that DT will always render.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:52.180
Another one is, again, if
you use expect snapshot file,

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:55.780
be careful about what's going
on with those snapshots.

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:58.720
All the snapshot does is
says, I agree with myself,

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.000
but it doesn't mean
that it's correct.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.460
So you need to at
least manually confirm

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:05.030
that snapshot is correct.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.430
And then if you do agree with
yourself, you are correct.

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:08.390
So that's good.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:11.170
But there's that little
logical loophole.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:11.900
Be safe.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:14.200
You can always use
manual expectations,

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:17.140
or explicit expectations,
such as expect equal because that

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.600
will always be truthful,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.990
if you're comparing it to a static.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:23.670
And then lastly,
minimize the number

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:27.510
of screen shots expectations.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.240
Screenshots are worth
a thousand tests.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.160
They do test everything,
but you can also

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:36.220
imagine that's a very high false
positive rate with 1,000 tests.

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:38.010
And so they're very
brittle, they're

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.510
open to a lot of issues.

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:43.680
Also you cannot
compare across operating

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:45.570
systems or R versions.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:49.200
So it gets very
unwieldy very quickly.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.570
So unless screenshots
are absolute,

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:56.190
I would argue maybe not do that.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:59.750
So recap shinytest2: regression
testing for your app.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.300
You want to keep that
behavior over time.

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:05.579
If you have time to rest, you
have time to record a test.

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:09.150
And then interactively view your
application whenever possible

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.339
if you run into trouble.

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:14.160
And hopefully Gandalf
will change his tune

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.480
from you shall not pass to...
You Pass.

